course: dictionary
lesson: 11249
title: Worry: a word with two jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Worry can be a verb or a noun. As a verb, it means to keep thinking about problems and feel uneasy: «Tegan worries her picture looks too small.» As a noun, it means the feeling itself, or a thing you worry about. The small words around it show you which job it is doing.
explain: Look for clues. In «Mia said "Okay, no worries" and didn't push», the words "no worries" are a short, friendly phrase. They mean "that's fine", so there is nothing to fret about. Here worries is a noun inside a set phrase.
explain: Worry can also mean to make someone feel uneasy. «It worries me» means it makes me feel uneasy. When someone says «Don't worry», they are trying to comfort you. A good check is to swap in a phrase like "feel uneasy about" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Worry is a verb (worry, worries, worried, worrying) and a noun (a worry, worries).
fact: «Worried» means you keep thinking about a problem and feel uneasy.
fact: Worry can also be a noun for the feeling itself, as in «worry can make your tummy ache».
fact: A worry box, or worry jar, is a place to put your worries so you can share them with a trusted adult.
fact: Everyone worries sometimes, and talking or writing it down can help.
exercise: «Tegan worries her picture looks too small.» What does worries mean here? | *Feels uneasy about something | Draws a bigger picture | Forgets to finish | Makes a loud noise
exercise: In Australia, «no worries» is an informal way of saying something. What does it mean? | I have lost my way | *That's fine | Dangerous weather | Please hurry up
exercise: What is a worry box? | A box for keeping old toys | *A place to put your worries so you can share them with a trusted adult | A box for drawing pictures
test: In «Don't worry», which word class is worry? | Noun | Adverb | Adjective | *Verb
test: Which phrase describes a noun meaning a thing you feel uneasy about? | Worry box | Worried about | Worrying loudly | *A big worry
exam: What does «It worries me» mean? | It makes me go fast | *It makes me feel uneasy | It makes me tired and sleepy | It makes me laugh
